---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-ibd`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: "Vitamin D and Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Crohn's & Ulcerative Colitis)",
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [
    { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: "Crohn's disease", code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'K50', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } },
    { '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Ulcerative colitis', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'K51', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } },
  ],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and IBD', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and IBD: Crohn's, Ulcerative Colitis, Relapse Risk and Dosing"
  description="Vitamin D deficiency is very common in IBD (up to 70% in active Crohn's). What supplementation trials show for relapse prevention and disease activity — with practical dosing."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-ibd", label: "Vitamin D & IBD" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Inflammatory Bowel Disease</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Inflammatory bowel disease — Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis — sits at the
      intersection of a disease with a strong latitude gradient, a population with high
      rates of vitamin D deficiency, and a biology (mucosal immunity, T-cell regulation,
      antimicrobial peptides) that vitamin D actively modulates.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Deficiency is the norm in IBD</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cross-sectional studies find 60–70% of adults with active Crohn's have 25(OH)D
      &lt; 20 ng/mL, and 40–50% of ulcerative colitis patients. Reasons stack: reduced
      intestinal absorption (especially with terminal ileal Crohn's or resection), reduced
      sun exposure during flares, corticosteroid effects on vitamin D metabolism, and
      dietary avoidance of dairy.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The latitude and MR evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      IBD incidence increases sharply with latitude — the Nurses' Health Study found women
      in the highest 25(OH)D quartile had 46% lower Crohn's incidence than those in the
      lowest. Mendelian randomisation studies using genetic variants that lower 25(OH)D
      suggest a causal link with Crohn's specifically, less clearly with ulcerative colitis.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Supplementation trials</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Jørgensen 2010</strong> — 94 Crohn's patients in remission; 1,200 IU cholecalciferol/day for 12 months reduced relapse rate from 29% to 13% (p = 0.06 — a trend, not conventional significance).</li>
      <li><strong>Yang 2013</strong> — 108 Crohn's; 5,000 IU/day raised 25(OH)D and reduced disease activity index vs standard care.</li>
      <li><strong>Cochrane 2023 review</strong> — very low-certainty evidence for reducing clinical relapse in Crohn's; insufficient data for UC. Well-powered trials are still lacking.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical management</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Everyone with IBD should have 25(OH)D checked at diagnosis and annually.</li>
      <li>Target 25(OH)D 30–50 ng/mL (75–125 nmol/L).</li>
      <li>Malabsorption often requires larger doses (2,000–5,000 IU/day) than in the general population; some patients need 10,000 IU/day.</li>
      <li>Terminal ileal Crohn's, short-bowel syndrome, or post-resection patients may need 25(OH)D (calcifediol) or IM cholecalciferol if oral is inadequate.</li>
      <li>Check calcium at higher doses to avoid iatrogenic hypercalcaemia.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Vitamin D is an adjunct in IBD — not a substitute
      for 5-ASA, immunomodulators, biologics, or JAK inhibitors as clinically indicated.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-ibd" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
